{
  "raw": {
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317356": {
      "title": "Associate Professor of Collaborative Piano, College of Fine Arts, School of Music",
      "description": "Associate Professor of Collaborative Piano, College of Fine Arts, School of Music - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Associate Professor of Collaborative Piano, College of Fine Arts, School of Music - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Associate Professor of Collaborative Piano, College of Fine Arts, School of Music ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937830930 Posted Date 9/30/2026 Minimum Salary $114,000.00 Maximum Salary $131,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular Boston University, College of Fine Arts, School of Music Associate Professor of Music; Applied Collaborative Piano The School of Music at Boston University’s College of Fine Arts seeks to hire an experienced collaborative piano teacher and performer as an associate professor. An appointment is sought to begin July 1, 2027. This position requires excellent applied teaching skills and a professional performance profile of national recognition. The successful candidate will lead an established collaborative piano degree program in the areas of instrumental and vocal music; teach lessons, mentor and advise MM and DMA collaborative piano students; oversee all collaborative piano curricula; organize graduate assistant collaborative partnership assignments; coach chamber ensembles; and teach courses in sonata repertoire and vocal coaching. Key Job Responsibilities: Recruit and teach a studio of graduate students in collaborative piano Teach courses in vocal coaching and collaborative repertoire, vocal and instrumental Serve as academic advisor to all collaborative piano majors Facilitate collaborative piano assignments with faculty and students across all ensemble and applied areas of the School Maintain a national performance profile The candidate will have a record of successful teaching at the collegiate-conservatory level, a nationally recognized performance profile, and the ability to recruit high-level collaborative piano majors at the MM and DMA levels. Refined skills in leadership, communication and organization are critical for a successful candidate who will work closely with staff and faculty colleagues to support collaborative piano majors as well as the students with whom they partner. Experience in successful interactions with faculty colleagues in opera, orchestra, wind ensemble, choral and all applied areas is essential. A terminal degree in music performance is required. This position is expected to be made at the level of associate professor (non-tenure track). The Boston University School of Music was the first institution in the United States to grant a degree in music. Today, the School of Music trains musicians, educators, composers, and scholars in strong undergraduate and graduate programs. Each year the School of Music presents hundreds of public concerts, recitals, operas, and other performances, as part of the greater resources and vibrant scholarly community of Boston University as a whole, and the wider intellectual and musical offerings of the Boston metropolitan area. Boston University is an AAU institution with a rich tradition dedicated to inclusion and social justice. We are proud to be the first American university to award a Ph.D. to a woman, and we celebrate our record of and commitment to inclusiveness. The College of Fine Arts includes diversity as one of its core strategic goals. The successful applicant will enjoy working in an inclusive school that values cultural and ethnic diversity and will contribute to ongoing efforts to promote equity for all people. Required Skills Qualified applicants should submit: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Phone and email addresses of three (3) professional references who may be contacted All application materials should be sent via email to musicsearch@bu.edu and must be received no later than November 1, 2026. Anticipated date of appointment: July 1, 2027 (pending budgetary approval). BU conducts a background check on all final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. The background check includes contacting the final candidate’s current and previous employer(s) to ask whether, in the last seven years, there has been a substantiated finding of misconduct violating that employer’s applicable sexual misconduct policies. To implement this process, the University requires a final candidate to complete and sign the form entitled “Authorization to Release Information” after execution of an offer letter. We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317252": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Lighting, College of Fine Arts, School of Theatre",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Lighting, College of Fine Arts, School of Theatre - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Assistant Professor of Lighting, College of Fine Arts, School of Theatre - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Assistant Professor of Lighting, College of Fine Arts, School of Theatre ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937640903 Posted Date 9/3/2026 Minimum Salary $90,000.00 Maximum Salary $100,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular Boston University (BU) invites applications for a faculty position at the rank of non-tenure track Assistant Professor of Lighting at the School of Theatre (SOT) at the College of Fine Arts (CFA). The BFA and MFA Lighting departments at BU are leading national programs in the field, with Niche citing Boston University as one of the “Best Colleges for Performing Arts” in 2025 (#17 out of 522), as well as a “Best College for Design” in 2025 (#10 out of 491) . The program and SOT are close-knit professional communities within a large tier 1 research university. We seek an artist-educator to join our faculty as a full-time Assistant Professor of Lighting Design. This is a 9‑month appointment on a three‑year renewable contract; non‑tenure‑track faculty make up the standard faculty model in the College of Fine Arts. We seek a creative, collaborative teacher who foregrounds artistic experimentation and design innovation while sustaining rigorous craft training and inclusive pedagogy. The successful candidate will mentor student designers across undergraduate and graduate programs, contribute to a lively production season, and help advance the School’s curricular goals and forward‑thinking approaches to design training. Required Skills Qualifications The candidate should possess expertise and knowledge of the techniques, design strategies, intellectual discourse, history and current practices of lighting for the stage. The successful candidate will be well-prepared to teach students at all levels (graduate and undergraduate) in studio and seminar courses, and be able to provide strong evidence of ability to collaborate with co-educators, as well as be able to provide evidence of an established or emerging national profile in lighting design. The successful candidate should also have a significant professional lighting design portfolio demonstrating artistic accomplishment, and should have demonstrative communication and administrative skills. An MFA in Lighting Design or a closely related field, or equivalent professional experience. The candidate is also expected to have: Strong production experience (in live theatre and if possible, dance) and evidence of impact in the field. A record of college-level/pre-professional teaching experience that demonstrates evidence of excellent mentorship and innovative pedagogy. Evidence of successful teaching or mentorship at the collegiate or pre‑professional level. Experience with digital lighting workflows and automated systems. Proficiency with industry software (e.g., Vectorworks, Lightwright, ETC EOS family) and familiarity with contemporary workflow best practices. Demonstrated commitment to inclusive pedagogy and mentoring students from diverse backgrounds. Active professional networks and a record of regional/national/international engagement. BU SOT prepares students to think critically and work collaboratively about theatre, its practice, and its impact on the communities it serves. The successful candidate should be able to foster excellence and creative agency from a culturally and artistically informed position. The ability to foster and build upon interdepartmental connections is a strength of BU and central to BU SOT’s mission as a theatre school within a major research university. The candidate will ideally demonstrate excellence in interdisciplinary teaching informed by both experimentation and research and be adept at building connections with other university programs, schools and centers. Boston University’s School of Theatre is a vibrant center for creative inquiry and professional preparation within a leading private research university. We offer rigorous BFA and MFA programs across performance, design, production, and management, blending hands‑on practice with critical study, collaborative projects, and interdisciplinary engagement. Our faculty are active artists and scholars with national and international profiles; students benefit from direct mentorship, regular public performances, and partnerships across the College of Fine Arts and the wider University. The School produces a lively, diversified season of productions in multiple performance spaces—ranging from intimate studio work to fully realized mainstage shows—giving students practical, real‑world experience in every stage of production. Our facilities include contemporary design studios and well‑equipped scene and costume workshops, supported by professional technical staff and student‑run crews. Situated in the cultural and theatrical hub of Boston, the School connects learners with a broad network of regional companies, festivals, and arts institutions, and maintains close collaborations with the School of Music. Our graduates are collaborative, reflective artists who combine creative rigor with professional readiness to help shape the future of performance. Applying and Materials and Review Process Please submit the following as a single PDF or clearly labeled files via the application portal or send materials to the business manager, Michael Swartz-Flores (swartzmc@bu.edu) : Cover letter describing teaching philosophy, creative practice, and fit with BU’s program Curriculum vitae Teaching statement (including approaches to mentorship, equity, and assessment) Portfolio of design work (digital link preferred) Contact information for three professional references Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applicants are encouraged to apply early. Finalists will be asked to participate in an in-person interview and to present a portfolio, give a sample lecture or critique, and meet with students and faculty; on‑campus visit expenses will be reimbursed. Preference will be given to applications received by November 1st, 2026, for first round interviews. Applications will be considered until the position is filled. We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317379": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Visual Art (Painting), College of Fine Arts, School of Visual Arts",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Visual Art (Painting), College of Fine Arts, School of Visual Arts - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Assistant Professor of Visual Art (Painting), College of Fine Arts, School of Visual Arts - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Assistant Professor of Visual Art (Painting), College of Fine Arts, School of Visual Arts ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937861006 Posted Date 10/6/2026 Minimum Salary $92,500.00 Maximum Salary $100,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular Assistant Professor of Visual Art (Painting) Anticipated start date: July 1, 2027 Position description: Boston University (BU) invites applications for a faculty position at the rank of non-tenure track Assistant Professor of Visual Art in Painting at the School of Visual Arts (SVA) at the College of Fine Arts (CFA). The BFA and MFA Painting departments at BU are leading national programs in the field, with MFA Painting ranked #6 in US News & World Report. The program and SVA are close-knit professional communities within a large tier 1 research university. We seek an artist and educator who will complement and strengthen current faculty in a program that empowers students to create work that is rigorous and exploratory in form and content. The successful candidate will be an emergent to established artist with an expertise in painting in the expanded field. The candidate’s professional practice may include adjacent areas of expertise. The candidate will have a versatile skill set and proficiency in contemporary, historical, conceptual, and material approaches to the medium. BU SVA candidates should expect to teach across the BFA and MFA levels, including Foundations and Non-Major courses. Evidence of the ability to teach students with diverse experiences and perspectives is required. Assistant Professors engage with School and College service. The position is a full-time non-tenure track Assistant Professorship, and BU offers excellent benefits and research support. BU SVA prepares students to think seriously, to see critically, to make intensely, and to act with creative agency from a culturally and artistically informed position. The ability to foster and build upon interdepartmental connections is a strength of BU and central to BU SVA’s mission as an art school within a major research university. The candidate will ideally demonstrate excellence in interdisciplinary teaching informed by both experimentation and research and be adept at building connections with other university programs and centers. Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the first universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. Please visit https://www.bu.edu/values/ to learn more about our shared values. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship, engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Required Skills Qualifications The candidate should possess expertise and knowledge of the materials, techniques, intellectual discourse, and history of their field informed by recent work and scholarship in visual art. The successful candidate will be well-prepared to teach students at all levels (graduate and undergraduate) in studio and seminar courses. The candidate should have a proven ability to teach the critical and conceptual frameworks of visual art. An MFA in Painting or a related area is required. The candidate is also expected to have: A strong exhibition record and evidence of impact in the field. A record of college-level teaching experience that demonstrates evidence of excellent mentorship and innovative pedagogy. To apply: Please upload in SlideRoom: a cover letter; an annotated academic CV with a professional website listed; names and contact information for 3 references; samples of creative work (up to 20 images); samples of student work (up to 20 images); and a teaching philosophy that expresses how teaching relates to one’s practice, and conveys strategies navigating varying experiences and perspectives in the classroom. Upload images to: http://bucfa.slideroom.com . No email applications please. Preference will be given to applications received by November 15, 2026, for first round interviews. Applications will be considered until the position is filled. Boston University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender, age, national origin, ethnicity, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law or identified in the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Retaliation is also prohibited. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Under Massachusetts law, we may not require or administer a lie detector test as a condition of employment or continued employment. Boston University, consistent with the AAU’s Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment, conducts background checks for final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. As part of this process, we ask current and past employers of the last seven years whether there has been a substantiated finding of sexual misconduct under their policies. To move forward, final candidates must complete and sign an “Authorization to Release Information” form after receiving an offer to enable this background check. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317274": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, College of Arts and Sciences, Department of Political Science",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, College of Arts and Sciences, Department of Political Science - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Assistant Professor, College of Arts and Sciences, Department of Political Science - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Assistant Professor, College of Arts and Sciences, Department of Political Science ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937680910 Posted Date 9/10/2026 Minimum Salary $115,000.00 Maximum Salary $130,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular Boston University invites applications for a faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor in the Department of Political Science. We seek candidates who specialize in the intersection of artificial intelligence and politics or who use artificial intelligence applications as a methodology for studying key questions in political science. The search is open with respect to subfield. The anticipated start date is July 1, 2027, pending final budgetary approval. Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the ﬁrst universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. Please visit https://www.bu.edu/values/ to learn more about our shared values. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship, engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Required Skills The successful candidate will teach graduate and undergraduate students, engage in service to the Department, College of Arts and Sciences, and University, and hold a PhD at the start of the appointment. The starting salary for this position is $115,000 to $130,000. To Apply: To ensure full consideration, please submit the following materials by October 1, 2026 via AcademicJobsOnline ( https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32578 ): cover letter, CV, research statement, teaching statement, sample of scholarly writing, and three letters of reference. Boston University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualiﬁed applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender, age, national origin, ethnicity, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law or identiﬁed in the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Retaliation is also prohibited. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Under Massachusetts law, we may not require or administer a lie detector test as a condition of employment or continued employment. Boston University, consistent with the AAU’s Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment, conducts background checks for ﬁnal candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. As part of this process, we ask current and past employers of the last seven years whether there has been a substantiated ﬁnding of sexual misconduct under their policies. To move forward, ﬁnal candidates must complete and sign an “Authorization to Release Information” form after receiving an offer to enable this background check. DO NOT APPLY THROUGH THE BOSTON UNIVERSITY HR WEBSITE. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
