    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317275": {
      "title": "Lecturer, College of Arts and Sciences, Department of Romance Studies",
      "description": "Lecturer, College of Arts and Sciences, Department of Romance Studies - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Lecturer, College of Arts and Sciences, Department of Romance Studies - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Lecturer, College of Arts and Sciences, Department of Romance Studies ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937690910 Posted Date 9/10/2026 Minimum Salary $72,000.00 Maximum Salary $72,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Department of Romance Studies at Boston University invites applications for a full-time Lecturer in French, beginning July 1, 2027. Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the ﬁrst universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. Please visit https://www.bu.edu/values/ to learn more about our shared values. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship, engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Required Skills Candidates must have superior to native oral and written proficiency in French and English, a Master’s degree or PhD in French, Second Language Acquisition, or a related field, and at least three years of college-level teaching experience. We seek individuals who demonstrate excellence in student-centered teaching and a commitment to inclusive and innovative language teaching practices. Responsibilities include teaching six courses per year, from beginning to advanced language and culture, coordinating multi-section language courses, leveraging digital technologies to enhance teaching and learning, and contributing to curriculum development in a dynamic and collaborative language program. Ability to teach Italian or Spanish would be an asset. The starting salary for this position is $72,000. The position offers a professional career track with possibilities for advancement. Please visit our website for more information about the Department of Romance Studies: https://www.bu.edu/rs/. To Apply: Please submit via https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32469 (1) a cover letter that explains your interest in and qualifications for the position, (2) an up-to-date curriculum vitae, (3) a statement of teaching philosophy that addresses your commitment to inclusive and innovative teaching, (4) a sample syllabus for a past or prospective course at the intermediate-high to advanced-mid level of language instruction, and (5) three letters of recommendation. Other materials (such as course evaluations, sample course materials, and/or a link to an unedited video of one of your class sessions) may be requested later in the interview process. Applications submitted through a method other than AcademicJobsOnline will not be considered. The application deadline is October 7. Boston University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualiﬁed applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender, age, national origin, ethnicity, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law or identiﬁed in the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Retaliation is also prohibited. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Under Massachusetts law, we may not require or administer a lie detector test as a condition of employment or continued employment. Boston University, consistent with the AAU’s Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment, conducts background checks for ﬁnal candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. As part of this process, we ask current and past employers of the last seven years whether there has been a substantiated ﬁnding of sexual misconduct under their policies. To move forward, ﬁnal candidates must complete and sign an “Authorization to Release Information” form after receiving an offer to enable this background check. DO NOT APPLY THROUGH THE BOSTON UNIVERSITY HR WEBSITE. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317276": {
      "title": "Lecturer, College of Arts and Sciences, Undergraduate Program in Neuroscience",
      "description": "Lecturer, College of Arts and Sciences, Undergraduate Program in Neuroscience - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Lecturer, College of Arts and Sciences, Undergraduate Program in Neuroscience - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Lecturer, College of Arts and Sciences, Undergraduate Program in Neuroscience ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937700910 Posted Date 9/10/2026 Minimum Salary $72,000.00 Maximum Salary $72,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Undergraduate Program in Neuroscience at Boston University invites applications for a full-time Lecturer beginning September 1, 2027. This is a teaching track line with possibility for renewal and promotion based on performance. We seek a colleague with a commitment to undergraduate education and a track record of excellence in teaching who has research expertise in any area of Drosophila neurobiology. Teaching responsibilities each academic year will include (1) teaching three lecture-based courses in the candidate’s area of expertise, and (2) teaching two laboratory sections and serving as Laboratory Director for the fall semester course NE 203 Principles of Neuroscience, where students employ transgenic approaches to investigate the neurobiology of Drosophila behavior. As NE 203 Laboratory Director, our new colleague will have the opportunity to build on our well-established NE 203 lab curriculum to incorporate techniques and research questions from their own area of expertise. The successful candidate will have a PhD in neuroscience, biology, or a related field and experience with evidence-based teaching practices and incorporating research into teaching. The starting salary for this position is $72,000. The position will involve active engagement in the academic life of the Undergraduate Program in Neuroscience, such as serving as an academic mentor to a cohort of undergraduate neuroscience majors, coordinating student peer-mentoring programs, developing curricular materials, serving as a research mentor for undergraduates, participating in undergraduate Honors, Master’s, or PhD committees, and contributing to the development of academic initiatives. The Undergraduate Program in Neuroscience believes that the cultural and social diversity of our faculty, staff, and students is vitally important to the distinction and excellence of our program. We are especially eager to have colleagues join our department who support our institutional commitment to ensuring BU is inclusive, equitable, and diverse. The successful candidate will join a close-knit cohort of faculty in the Undergraduate Program in Neuroscience that benefits from close affiliations with programs in Biology, Psychological & Brain Sciences, and other units. To Apply: Please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae and statement of teaching philosophy to https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32567 . Please arrange for three letters of reference to be submitted through the same website. Review of applications will begin December 15, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Inquiries about the position can be addressed to Dr. Mario Muscedere at mario@bu.edu. Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the first universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship, engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Boston University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender, age, national origin, ethnicity, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law or identified in the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Retaliation is also prohibited. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Under Massachusetts law, we may not require or administer a lie detector test as a condition of employment or continued employment. Boston University, consistent with the AAU’s Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment, conducts background checks for final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. As part of this process, we ask current and past employers of the last seven years whether there has been a substantiated finding of sexual misconduct under their policies. To move forward, final candidates must complete and sign an “Authorization to Release Information” form after receiving an offer to enable this background check. DO NOT APPLY THROUGH THE BOSTON UNIVERSITY HR WEBSITE This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317282": {
      "title": "Non-Tenure Track Clinical Assistant Professor in the Clinical Practice Department, School of Social Work",
      "description": "Non-Tenure Track Clinical Assistant Professor in the Clinical Practice Department, School of Social Work - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Non-Tenure Track Clinical Assistant Professor in the Clinical Practice Department, School of Social Work - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Non-Tenure Track Clinical Assistant Professor in the Clinical Practice Department, School of Social Work ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937730911 Posted Date 9/11/2026 Minimum Salary $95,000.00 Maximum Salary $105,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular Boston University invites applications for two non-tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of Clinical Assistant Professor in the School of Social Work in the Clinical Practice Department beginning Fall 2027. We invite persons with a strong commitment to justice focused on anti-oppressive practice, scholarship, and teaching who are licensed clinical social workers with significant direct practice experience. The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, and training. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. As one of the nation’s top schools of social work, Boston University School of Social Work (BUSSW) is dedicated to the pursuit of excellence in education, research, and evidence-based and anti-oppressive practice to solve the most pressing societal problems of the 21st century. We are particularly committed to having an impact in all aspects of health, for individuals, groups, families, and society as a whole—as evidenced by the dual degree we offer with BU’s School of Public Health, and by our three research centers (Center for Innovation in Social Work and Health (CISWH), Center for Aging and Disability Education and Research (CADER), and The Institute for Equity in Child Opportunity and Healthy Development (IECOHD)). Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the first universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. Please visit https://www.bu.edu/values/ to learn more about our shared values. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship, engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Required Skills Requirements: Candidates should have: An MSW degree with licensure Evidence of excellent teaching and/or training skills in areas relevant to clinical practice Preferred: PhD in social work or equivalent graduate degree Expertise in children, youth, and families Experience teaching high-enrollment core clinical courses across modalities Experience in anti-oppressive clinical assessment & interventions aligned with current student demand To Apply: Application materials must be sent to swsearch@bu.edu. The application deadline is November 20th or until the position is filled. Early applications are strongly encouraged. Applicants should submit one combined pdf including: 1) a cover letter which includes statements of research accomplishments and goals as well as teaching interests and experiences; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) two articles (or article-length writing samples); and 4) the names and contact information for three references. If possible, please provide at least one scholarly publication as a writing sample, and clarify your role on multi-author publications. In your cover letter, please describe your research agenda, teaching skills and experience, and practice experience, and (if applicable) describe how your work relates to the areas of interest detailed earlier in this announcement. In your cover letter, we welcome a description of how, as a teacher, researcher, scholar, and/or practitioner, you demonstrate a commitment to the mission and values of the social work profession and/or how you would support BU School of Social Work’s ongoing efforts towards the same. We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/316531": {
      "title": "Part-Time Pediatric and Neonatal ICU Physician's Assistant/Nurse Practitioner",
      "description": "Part-Time Pediatric and Neonatal ICU Physician's Assistant/Nurse Practitioner - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Part-Time Pediatric and Neonatal ICU Physician's Assistant/Nurse Practitioner - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Part-Time Pediatric and Neonatal ICU Physician's Assistant/Nurse Practitioner ﻿ Category Boston University Medical Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937130331 Posted Date 7/14/2026 Minimum Salary $151,265.25 Maximum Salary $167,618.25 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Part-Time About the Role: Boston Medical Center Department of Pediatrics is seeking Physician Assistants (PA) or Nurse Practitioners (NP) to be part of a team of Advance Practice Providers (PAs and NPs) who provide care under the supervision of an intensivist physician to critically-ill patients in both our Level III Neonatal ICU (22 beds) and Pediatric ICU (4 beds). The NICU is staffed 24/7 by an in-house intensivist providing direct supervision to APPs. The PICU is staffed by pediatric intensivists who are in-house during the day and may be at home or in-house overnight depending on acuity and the needs of the patients and unit. APPs overlap with Boston Children's combined residents to provide frontline care to our patients. We are seeking energetic team players to join our staff. Experience in the NICU or PICU is not required, but preferred. We are currently recruiting for a part-time position. Clinical shifts are centered around nights and weekends. Following training in both the NICU and PICU, the APP will be assigned shifts in both locations. At no time will an APP care for patients in both units during a single shift. Key Responsibilities: Interviewing patients and families and performing physical examination (including obtaining, updating, and studying medical histories). Developing a problem list, differential diagnosis and treatment. Recommending options and courses of action for patient care Determining abnormal conditions by administering or ordering diagnostic test, such as X-rays and laboratory studies and by interpreting test results. Placing and modifying care orders Completing documentation for admission, daily and discharge notes Interfacing with multidisciplinary team members (i.e. nursing, respiratory therapy, dietary, case management, etc.) NICU: attend deliveries alone and with the NICU attending. Provide consultations for Newborn Nursery as requested. Implementing physician directives Rounding with team Provide ongoing patient assessment and intervention Consulting specialty and surgical services under direction of attending MD Coordinating discharge including follow-up appointments, equipment and discharge teaching Performing therapeutic procedures appropriate to unit following training (i.e. central line placement and removal, arterial line placement, chest tube placement, endotracheal intubation, procedural sedation) Protecting patients and employees by adhering to infection-control policies and protocols Comply with federal, state, and local legal and professional requirements Participating in continuing education, quality improvement and patient safety initiatives, educational conferences and research studies Responding to Pediatric Rapid Responses and Pediatric Codes outside the PICU/NICU Participating in unit patients safety and quality improvement initiatives Keeping abreast of advances in patient care Required Skills Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree Graduate of an accredited and/or approved nurse practitioner or physician assistant program Physicians Assistance or Nurse Practitioner licensure within the first 3 months following the receipt of a complete application for medical staff membership and privileges Physician Assistant or Nurse Practitioner Certification Basic Life Support (BLS), Neonatal Resuscitation Program (NRP) and Pediatric Advanced Life Support (PALS) certifications Previous pediatric experience preferred Must demonstrate knowledge and skills necessary to assess neonates through young adults Interpersonal skills needed for interacting effectively with physicians, staff, patients and families Compensation: Boston University Chobanian & Avedisian School of Medicine (BU-CASM) offers a competitive salary commensurate with academic training and practice experience. We continue to evaluate our compensation plan to remain one of the most competitive in salary and benefits. Our staff receive a highly competitive salary and generous benefits that include paid vacation, sick time, parental leave, and CME expenses. Faculty members and their dependents qualify for greatly reduced tuition at Boston University, ranked among the top 50 universities in the US. Compensation ranges between $151,265.25 and $167,618.25 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training, and departmental budget. The above hiring range represents a good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. How to Apply : If interested in this employment opportunity, please send your resume/CV and cover letter directly to: PediatricsFacultyAffairs@bmc.org About Us: Boston Medical Center proudly serves a diverse population in Boston and boasts a world-class academic medical center in Boston University School of Medicine, with a commitment to caring for all patients, including the underserved. BMC is a 514-bed academic medical center located in Boston’s historic South End. The largest safety-net hospital in New England, BMC provides consistently excellent and accessible health care to all. BMC is the primary teaching affiliate of BU-CASM, a highly ranked medical school dedicated to urban and international health and a recognized leader in groundbreaking medical research. Boston Medical Center is the academic teaching hospital for Boston University School of Medicine. Boston is an exciting place to live with everything a major world city can offer. The “smallest big city” in the US, enjoy watching the Patriots, Celtics, Bruins, and Red Sox, then hop a flight to Paris at Logan International Airport, just 10 minutes from downtown. Boston is also the nation’s healthcare hub and offers dramatic opportunities for innovation and academic advancement in the healthcare industry. Bostonians enjoy surrounding parks, beaches, and forest preserves by day, and local breweries, eclectic restaurants, historic theaters, and world-class music and arts by night, including the Boston Symphony Orchestra, the Museum of Fine Arts, and the Boston Ballet. With the most Colleges and Universities in the country, Boston feels young, cutting-edge, and highly academic. Boston Medical Center and Boston University comply with all state, federal, and local laws and regulations. Boston Medical Center and Boston University are equal opportunity employers, committed to a common mission of improving the health of Boston’s residents while adhering to the highest standards of academic medicine. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Boston University conducts a background check on all final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. The background check includes contacting the final candidate’s current and previous employer(s) to ask whether, in the last seven years, there has been a substantiated finding of misconduct violating that employer’s applicable sexual misconduct policies. To implement this process, the University requires a final candidate to complete and sign the form entitled “Authorization to Release Information” after the execution of an offer letter. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317324": {
      "title": "Professor of the Practice of Global Economic Development Policy, Pardee School",
      "description": "Professor of the Practice of Global Economic Development Policy, Pardee School - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Professor of the Practice of Global Economic Development Policy, Pardee School - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Professor of the Practice of Global Economic Development Policy, Pardee School ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937780921 Posted Date 9/21/2026 Minimum Salary $132,000.00 Maximum Salary $150,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Pardee School of Global Studies at Boston University invites applications for appointment as Professor of the Practice of Global Economic Development Policy, with a specialization in the economics of Eastern Europe, Central Asia, and/or South Asia. Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the first universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. Please visit https://www.bu.edu/values/ to learn more about our shared values. We seek scholars and practitioners who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship, engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Required Skills The successful candidate will be an international economist and distinguished practitioner who has worked at high levels in a major international organization or non-governmental organization. We are particularly interested in candidates whose work has focused on issues of institutional development, human capital, international trade or investment, infrastructure, or technology and innovation. The professor of the practice will teach courses in their areas of expertise, and will also advise and mentor students pursuing professional careers in international relations. Prior teaching experience and a record of publication and research will be considered a plus. To Apply: Please submit 1) a letter of application describing your teaching and research interests and professional experience, 2) curriculum vitae, and 3) three letters of recommendation electronically at https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32698. Questions about the position or school may be directed to Ms. Elaine Bidianos at elaineb@bu.edu. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, beginning October 23, 2026, but applications will be accepted until the job is filled. The expected starting date for employment is July 1, 2027. The salary range for this position is $132,000-150,000 Boston University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender, age, national origin, ethnicity, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law or identified in the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Retaliation is also prohibited. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Under Massachusetts law, we may not require or administer a lie detector test as a condition of employment or continued employment. Boston University, consistent with the AAU’s Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment, conducts background checks for final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. As part of this process, we ask current and past employers of the last seven years whether there has been a substantiated finding of sexual misconduct under their policies. To move forward, final candidates must complete and sign an “Authorization to Release Information” form after receiving an offer to enable this background check. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317273": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Department of Human Behavior, Research, and Policy, School of Social Work",
      "description": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Department of Human Behavior, Research, and Policy, School of Social Work - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Department of Human Behavior, Research, and Policy, School of Social Work - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Department of Human Behavior, Research, and Policy, School of Social Work ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937660909 Posted Date 9/10/2026 Minimum Salary $95,000.00 Maximum Salary $115,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular Boston University invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor in the School of Social Work in our Department of Human Behavior, Research, and Policy beginning Fall 2027. We invite applicants with a strong commitment to justice focused, anti-oppressive practice, scholarship, and teaching whose research focuses on children, youth, and families. We are especially interested in scholars working in one or more of the following areas: child welfare and child protection; school social work and school-based services; early childhood development and early care and education; children’s mental and behavioral health; and child, youth, and family policy. Strong quantitative analytic skills are central to this position. We seek candidates with demonstrated expertise in advanced methods and experience working with complex or large-scale datasets, who are prepared to teach and mentor students in quantitative research methods at the MSW and doctoral levels. We welcome scholarship that pairs quantitative rigor with qualitative or community-engaged approaches, but quantitative expertise is a core expectation. The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, and training. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. As one of the nation’s top schools of social work, Boston University School of Social Work (BUSSW) is dedicated to the pursuit of excellence in education, research, and evidence-based and anti-oppressive practice to solve the most pressing societal problems of the 21st century. We are particularly committed to having an impact in all aspects of health, for individuals, groups, families, and society as a whole—as evidenced by the dual degree we offer with BU’s School of Public Health, and by our three research centers (Center for Innovation in Social Work and Health (CISWH), Center for Aging and Disability Education and Research (CADER), and The Institute for Equity in Child Opportunity and Healthy Development (IECOHD)). Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the first universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship, engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Required Skills Requirements: Candidates should have: a doctoral degree in social work or related field (e.g., sociology, psychology, public health, public policy) by the time of appointment demonstrated expertise in advanced quantitative methods an active research agenda, evidence of scholarly productivity, and promise of future research funding Preferred: MSW degree Experience teaching quantitative methods or policy courses critical to doctoral and MSW curricula Research focus in one or more of the priority areas described above including their health and behavioral health dimensions To Apply: Application materials must be sent to swsearch@bu.edu. The application deadline is October 15th or until the position is filled. Early applications are strongly encouraged. Applicants should submit one combined pdf including: 1) a cover letter which includes statements of research accomplishments and goals as well as teaching interests and experiences; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) two articles (or article-length writing samples); and 4) the names and contact information for three references. If possible, please provide at least one scholarly publication as a writing sample, and clarify your role on multi-author publications. In your cover letter, please describe your research agenda, teaching skills and experience, and practice experience, and (if applicable) describe how your work relates to the areas of interest detailed earlier in this announcement. In your cover letter, we welcome a description of how, as a teacher, researcher, scholar, and/or practitioner, you demonstrate a commitment to the mission and values of the social work profession and/or how you would support BU School of Social Work’s ongoing efforts towards the same. We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317210": {
      "title": "Visiting Lecturer & Clinical Instructor, Boston University School of Law",
      "description": "Visiting Lecturer & Clinical Instructor, Boston University School of Law - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Visiting Lecturer & Clinical Instructor, Boston University School of Law - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Visiting Lecturer & Clinical Instructor, Boston University School of Law ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937600825 Posted Date 8/25/2026 Minimum Salary $100,000.00 Maximum Salary $125,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular Boston University invites applications for a faculty position at the rank of Visiting Lecturer & Clinical Instructor in the School of Law. BU Law invites immediate applications from candidates for a full‐time Visiting Lecturer & Clinical Instructor to serve in its BU/MIT Student Innovations Law Clinic (SILC), SILC is a unique collaboration between BU Law and the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, where BU Law students represent currently enrolled BU and MIT students on matters related to their innovative academic and extra-curricular activities. Clients routinely present cutting-edge questions in the areas of intellectual property, information privacy, cybersecurity, media law, and regulatory issues in health, finance, and other sectors. The Visiting Lecturer & Clinical Instructor will teach in either the Clinic’s Intellectual Property Practice Group or in its Privacy, Health, and Media Practice Group, which focus on issues related to copyright, trademark, patent, trade secret, defamation, fair advertising, consumer privacy, cybersecurity, government surveillance, platform liability, medical device and other health laws and regulations, and related areas of law. Working closely with the other experiential faculty, they will teach and supervise law students engaged in direct representation of clients. This search is for a one-to-two-year term Visiting Lecturer & Clinical Instructor position. The projected start date is August 31, 2026 or as soon as practical. This a is non-renewable, non-voting appointment not on the tenure track. However, the successful applicant will remain eligible to apply for any permanent positions that open during their term. The hired faculty member will be responsible for teaching in the clinic’s seminar on topics related to effective lawyering skills and substantive law, providing supervision to law students on a broad range of related topics, engaging with broader policy issues in the field of intellectual property; and participating actively in the administration of the Clinic’s law practice. Candidates should have proven experience and strong research and writing skills and should work well as part of a team. Important qualities include outstanding interpersonal skills, adept management of a large client docket with varying needs and issues, and a passion for work with creative and innovative clients. The ability to work sensitively with a diverse population of clients, students, and staff is essential. Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the ﬁrst universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. Please visit https://www.bu.edu/values/ to learn more about our shared values. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship, engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Boston University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualiﬁed applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender, age, national origin, ethnicity, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law or identiﬁed in the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Retaliation is also prohibited. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Under Massachusetts law, we may not require or administer a lie detector test as a condition of employment or continued employment. Boston University, consistent with the AAU’s Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment, conducts background checks for ﬁnal candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. As part of this process, we ask current and past employers of the last seven years whether there has been a substantiated ﬁnding of sexual misconduct under their policies. To move forward, ﬁnal candidates must complete and sign an “Authorization to Release Information” form after receiving an offer to enable this background check. Required Skills The ideal candidate for this position is an active member of a state bar and possesses expertise in a relevant area of law and an interest in the intersection of law, technology, and policy. Experience with clinical pedagogy, teaching, and supervision of students or junior attorneys is also a strong plus. Applicants with experience representing students, early-stage startups, academics, nonprofits, or technology-focused clients are also preferred. Entry-level candidates are welcome. Application Instructions: Applicants should send a letter of interest and a resume to Jef Pearlman, Director and Clinical Professor, Boston University School of Law, 765 Commonwealth Avenue, Boston, Massachusetts 02215. E-mail applications are encouraged and should be sent to Marielle O’Malley at mjom@bu.edu.. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, starting as soon as the position opens. To receive the most serious consideration of one's application, a candidate should send their application as soon as possible. All open faculty positions are pending budgetary approval. To learn more about the law school, visit our website at www.bu.edu/law. We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317300": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate, Arthritis and Autoimmune Diseases Center",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate, Arthritis and Autoimmune Diseases Center - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Postdoctoral Associate, Arthritis and Autoimmune Diseases Center - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Postdoctoral Associate, Arthritis and Autoimmune Diseases Center ﻿ Category Boston University Medical Campus --> Postdoctoral Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937740915 Posted Date 9/15/2026 Minimum Salary $68,000.00 Maximum Salary $68,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular A Research Scientist/Postdoctoral fellow position is available in the laboratory of Dr. Giovanni Ligresti to study basic and translational aspects of lung fibrosis with specific emphasis on molecular mechanisms regulating endothelial and fibroblast responses to lung injury. The work will be conducted within a multidisciplinary laboratory featuring state-of-the-art resources, cell/tissue/animal models, and access to excellent core facilities. Required Skills The successful candidate will have established experience in three or more of the following: (a) advanced techniques in biochemistry, cellular and molecular biology; (b) experimental modeling of fibrosis in mice; (c) primary cell culture and/or analysis of lung cell populations; (d) cell/tissue imaging techniques such as fluorescence, confocal, multiphoton microscopy (e) FACS sorting and analysis; (f) Bioinformatics. The candidate will be expected to develop and execute independent research plans leading to peer-reviewed publications, should be comfortable working within an interdisciplinary group of research personnel, and possess excellent oral and written communication skills. Maximum consideration will be given to candidates with strong research motivation and initiative towards independence. Although the candidate's salary will be fully supported by intramural as well as extramural (NIH) funding, he/she will be expected to initiate progress towards independence via submission of research grant proposals. To apply: Please send application materials, including curriculum vitae and names of three or more references to: Giovanni Ligresti (ligresti@bu.edu) We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317378": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate, Epidemiology, School of Public Health",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate, Epidemiology, School of Public Health - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Postdoctoral Associate, Epidemiology, School of Public Health - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Postdoctoral Associate, Epidemiology, School of Public Health ﻿ Category Boston University Medical Campus --> Postdoctoral Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937851006 Posted Date 10/6/2026 Minimum Salary $67,500.00 Maximum Salary $85,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular ABOUT THE OPPORTUNITY The Department of Epidemiology at the Boston University School of Public Health invites applications for a Postdoctoral Associate Research Fellow to conduct NIH-funded epidemiologic research on the effects of environmental chemical exposures on reproductive health. The Postdoc will work with data from the Study of Environment, Lifestyle, and Fibroids (SELF), a prospective cohort study of Black women designed to investigate environmental, lifestyle, and reproductive factors associated with gynecologic and reproductive outcomes. They will lead and collaborate on analyses evaluating associations between environmental chemical exposures, including exposure to mixtures of chemicals, and reproductive outcomes, with a particular emphasis on fertility outcomes. This position provides an opportunity to develop expertise at the intersection of reproductive epidemiology, environmental epidemiology, and advanced statistical methods. The Postdoc will be expected to work independently and take intellectual leadership in statistical analysis, manuscript preparation, and dissemination of findings, while collaborating with investigators across epidemiology, environmental health, biostatistics, and reproductive health. A particular focus of the fellowship will be the application of mixtures modeling approaches to characterize the reproductive effects of correlated and co-occurring environmental chemical exposures. JOB SUMMARY The Postdoctoral Associate will conduct independent and collaborative epidemiologic research examining the effects of environmental chemical exposures on reproductive health outcomes. Using data from the SELF cohort, they will investigate associations between individual and combined chemical exposures and outcomes including fertility and other reproductive outcomes. The Postdoc is expected to demonstrate methodological rigor, intellectual initiative, and the ability to communicate quantitative findings to different audiences. The position will provide opportunities to publish first-authored and co-authored manuscripts in peer-reviewed journals, present findings at national conferences, and contribute to grant applications. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACCOUNTABILITIES 1. Design and conduct epidemiologic analyses using data from the Study of Environment, Lifestyle, and Fibroids (SELF) cohort. 2. Evaluate associations between environmental chemical exposures and reproductive outcomes. 3. Apply and extend advanced statistical methods for evaluating the health effects of chemical mixtures and correlated environmental exposures. 4. Develop analytic approaches that address exposure correlation, nonlinearity, interactions, and potential confounding in environmental epidemiologic studies. 5. Lead the preparation and submission of manuscripts for peer-reviewed journals. 6. Present research findings at national and international scientific conferences. 7. Contribute to the development of grant applications focused on environmental and reproductive health. 8. Collaborate with multidisciplinary investigators in epidemiology, environmental health, biostatistics, and reproductive health. 9. Contribute to data management, quality control, analytic planning, and interpretation of findings. 10. Mentor and support students in epidemiologic methods, statistical programming, and scientific writing. RESEARCH ENVIRONMENT The Postdoc will join an active research program in reproductive and environmental epidemiology at Boston University School of Public Health. The position offers opportunities to work with rich prospective cohort data and to develop expertise in innovative statistical approaches for environmental mixtures. The Postdoc will have opportunities for close mentorship and collaboration while also being encouraged to pursue research questions aligned with their interests and career goals. Required Skills QUALIFICATIONS Required 1. Doctoral degree (PhD, ScD, or equivalent) in Epidemiology, Environmental Health, or a closely related discipline. 2. Strong training in epidemiologic methods and quantitative research. 3. Demonstrated experience with environmental mixtures modeling, including methods for evaluating health effects of multiple correlated chemical exposures. 4. Experience conducting analyses using large observational or longitudinal epidemiologic datasets. 5. Strong programming and statistical skills in SAS, R, or a comparable statistical programming language. 6. Demonstrated ability to independently design, conduct, and interpret epidemiologic analyses. 7. Excellent scientific writing and oral communication skills. 8. Strong interest in reproductive and environmental health research. Preferred 1. Experience in environmental epidemiology, reproductive epidemiology, infertility research. 2. Experience with environmental chemical exposures, such as PFAS, heavy metals, persistent pesticides, or other endocrine-disrupting chemicals. 3. Familiarity with advanced methods for mixtures analysis, such as quantile g-computation, Bayesian kernel machine regression, weighted quantile sum regression, or related approaches. 4. Experience with time-to-event analyses of health outcomes. 5. Knowledge of causal inference methods, including directed acyclic graphs, multiple imputation, marginal structural models, and mediation analysis. 6. Experience analyzing longitudinal data. 7. Grant writing experience 8. Record of peer-reviewed publications commensurate with career stage. To apply: Interested candidates should submit the below documents to Professor Lauren Wise ( lwise@bu.edu ): 1. A cover letter describing their research background and interest in the position; 2. A curriculum vitae; 3. A statement of research interests describing relevant experience in epidemiology, environmental health, reproductive health, and/or mixtures modeling; and 4. Contact information for 3 references. Applications will be reviewed on an ongoing basis. ADDITIONAL INFORMATION Boston University is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Salary and benefits will be consistent with Boston University policies and NIH postdoctoral salary guidelines, as applicable. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/315655": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate, Pharmacology, Physiology & Biophysics, Chobanian & Avedisian School of Medicine",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate, Pharmacology, Physiology & Biophysics, Chobanian & Avedisian School of Medicine - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Postdoctoral Associate, Pharmacology, Physiology & Biophysics, Chobanian & Avedisian School of Medicine - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Postdoctoral Associate, Pharmacology, Physiology & Biophysics, Chobanian & Avedisian School of Medicine ﻿ Category Boston University Medical Campus --> Postdoctoral Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 25599936300718 Posted Date 7/31/2026 Minimum Salary $65,000.00 Maximum Salary $70,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The TCW laboratory ( https://sites.bu.edu/tcwlab/ ) in the Department of Pharmacology, Physiology & Biophysics at Boston University Chobanian & Avedisian School of Medicine in Boston, Massachusetts is looking for highly motivated and skilled stem cell biologists or electrophysiologists to be the drivers of our efforts to translate the genetics of human AD toward therapeutic interventions. The core program is to translate AD genetics and functional genomics into human brain cells both in vitro 2D and 3D models using human induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) from population and isogenic lines generated by CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing and in vivo hiPSC/rodent chimera systems in comparison with post-mortem brain. In particular, the research will focus on deciphering the molecular mechanism of major AD risk variants as well as protective variants, associated genes and identifying targeted therapeutics. Furthermore, the research focusing on human iPSC-glial transplantation in AD animal models followed by in vivo electrophysiological and behavior readouts will enable potential stem cell therapeutics. The candidate will join a multidisciplinary and highly collaborative research team that includes computational geneticists, neuroscientists and pharmacologists in the department as well as Genome Science Institute and Center for Systems Neuroscience at Boston University. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter outlining their research interests and career goals, a current CV, and contact information for three references to Dr. Julia TCW, Assistant professor and Director [juliatcw@bu.edu] or https://sites.bu.edu/tcwlab/talent-aquisition/ . Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Required Skills Experienced in handling stem cells (mouse or human iPSCs, embryonic stem cells) and brain cell differentiation (2D or 3D cell cultures), or Experienced in electrophysiology (in vivo ephys, Ca imaging, MEA, path clamping), animal behaviors (Plus) CRISPRa/i screens, genome-editing Clear written and oral communication and detailed documentation skills, with the ability to collaborate effectively across disciplines We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/316220": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate, Pulmonary Center, Department of Medicine",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate, Pulmonary Center, Department of Medicine - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Postdoctoral Associate, Pulmonary Center, Department of Medicine - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Postdoctoral Associate, Pulmonary Center, Department of Medicine ﻿ Category Boston University Medical Campus --> Postdoctoral Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 25599936751218 Posted Date 6/8/2026 Minimum Salary $67,500.00 Maximum Salary $75,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Department of Medicine at Boston University, Bosmann Laboratory, is recruiting highly motivated Postdoctoral Fellows to work on projects in Immunology and Host–Pathogen Interactions. The immune system protects against invading pathogens and opportunistic microbes at barrier surfaces such as the lung and gut. Dysregulation of these interactions plays a critical role in bacterial infections (including pneumonia and sepsis) as well as inflammatory bowel disease. In the absence of fully effective therapeutic options, these microbe-driven inflammatory diseases represent a major and growing burden on health care systems worldwide. Our laboratory is dedicated to achieving a mechanistic understanding of the molecular pathogenesis of bacterial infections and host–pathogen interactions, with the goal of identifying and evaluating novel therapeutic targets. Please see our publication in Nature Communications. 2020 Aug 12;11(1):4035. A particular emphasis is placed on novel genes, for which our laboratory has generated multiple genetically engineered mouse models. We seek outstanding postdoctoral candidates with strong career potential to advance our experimental and translational research programs. Depending on the interests and background of the trainee, projects may focus on IBD-related mucosal immunity or on pneumonia and sepsis, or on shared immune mechanisms across these disease contexts. Key areas of interest include: Bacterial polyphosphates in immunity at barrier organs (gut and lung) Host–pathogen interactions Innate immune cells, including macrophages, neutrophils and T cells Cytokine biology and immune signaling pathways (e.g. G-protein signaling) Pathogens of interest include Escherichia coli, Streptococcus pneumoniae, Legionella spp., and others. Projects will be individually tailored to align with the qualifications and scientific interests of the postdoctoral fellow. The research environment offers access to a broad spectrum of state-of-the-art immunological techniques, including single-cell transcriptomics (scRNA-Seq, TOTAL-Seq) and phospho-/proteomics. Data analysis is supported by an experienced bioinformatician within the laboratory. Further information is available here: https://sites.bu.edu/bosmannlab/ Required Skills Preferred candidates will have prior experience in experimental immunology, mouse models of infection or inflammation, innate immunity, and/or transcriptomic or proteomic approaches. TO APPLY: Please send your CV, publication list, transcripts and names of three references to: Markus Bosmann (mbosmann@bu.edu), M.D., Associate Professor of Medicine, Pathology & Laboratory Medicine, Investigator of the National Emerging Infectious Diseases Laboratories We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317344": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate, Center for Brain Recovery, Sargent College of Health and Rehabilitation Services",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate, Center for Brain Recovery, Sargent College of Health and Rehabilitation Services - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Postdoctoral Associate, Center for Brain Recovery, Sargent College of Health and Rehabilitation Services - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Postdoctoral Associate, Center for Brain Recovery, Sargent College of Health and Rehabilitation Services ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Postdoctoral Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937810929 Posted Date 9/29/2026 Minimum Salary $67,500.00 Maximum Salary $67,500.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Center for Brain Recovery (CBR) at Boston University is a cutting-edge research center focused on understanding mechanisms of language recovery following stroke and developing effective treatments for stroke survivors. Our interdisciplinary team of clinicians, neuroscientists, engineers, and data scientists is dedicated to advancing precision rehabilitation and next-generation neurotechnologies for individuals with aphasia. Position Overview The Center for Brain Recovery is seeking a highly motivated and innovative Postdoctoral Researcher to join our dynamic team working on two complementary research initiatives. The successful candidate will advance groundbreaking work in understanding how the brain recovers language function after stroke and in developing neuroimaging-based prognostic frameworks to enhance precision rehabilitation, neuromodulation and therapeutics for aphasia. This position offers a unique opportunity to work on cutting-edge research in neuroimaging, computational neuroscience, and precision therapeutics for aphasia recovery. Key Responsibilities Neural Network Engagement & Language Recovery Research: Conduct and analyze fMRI studies investigating how different brain networks (language network and domain-general multiple demand network) support language processing in persons with aphasia Evaluate how intensive language therapy modulates neural engagement across brain networks Neuroimaging-Based Prognostication & Connectivity Mapping: Develop and refine computational pipelines for analyzing directional temporal-frontal connectivity using advanced neuroimaging data (MRI, DTI, rsfMRI) Build and validate lesion-informed cortical-dynamics simulations to compute patient-specific effective connectivity Create personalized \"digital twins\" of patient brains to identify recruitable language function and viable neuroprosthesis targets Computational Modeling & Machine Learning: Develop machine learning models that predict individual recovery prognosis and treatment response from multimodal neuroimaging and behavioral data Apply interpretable AI methods to identify predictive neural biomarkers Validate prognostic algorithms on independent cohorts and translate findings into clinical candidacy assessments Clinical Research & Data Management: Coordinate recruitment, screening, and enrollment of stroke survivors with aphasia across varying severity levels Oversee fMRI data collection and quality assurance Manage longitudinal study databases and ensure compliance with ethical research standards Contribute to clinical characterization of participants and behavioral assessments Required Skills Qualifications Required: Ph.D. in neuroscience, neuroimaging, computational neuroscience, biomedical engineering, data science, psychology, or related field Strong experience with fMRI data acquisition and analysis Proficiency in neuroimaging analysis software (FSL, SPM, AFNI) and programming languages (Python, MATLAB, or R) Experience with structural connectivity analysis and/or computational modeling Excellent written and verbal communication skills Ability to work collaboratively in an interdisciplinary team Commitment to ethical research practices and patient-centered care Highly Desired: Experience with machine learning or deep learning applications in neuroimaging Prior involvement in clinical trials or clinical research with stroke or aphasia populations Experience with network neuroscience or connectivity analyses Familiarity with brain-computer interface, neuromodulation research or neurorehabilitation Please note: this position is contingent upon external grant funding and is subject to annual review. To apply Interested candidates should submit the following documents to brainrec@bu.edu or kirans@bu.edu: A cover letter (1 page) outlining your research interests, relevant experience, and why this position aligns with your career goals A current CV detailing your academic and research achievements Contact information for at least two professional references We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317323": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate, Mechanical Engineering Department, College of Engineering",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate, Mechanical Engineering Department, College of Engineering - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Postdoctoral Associate, Mechanical Engineering Department, College of Engineering - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Postdoctoral Associate, Mechanical Engineering Department, College of Engineering ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Postdoctoral Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937770921 Posted Date 9/21/2026 Minimum Salary $67,500.00 Maximum Salary $80,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Mesostructured Materials and Devices Lab at Boston University led by Prof. Joerg Werner is looking for one or more postdoctoral associates to join a team of interdisciplinary researchers developing and establishing an advanced battery cell technology. Successful candidates are entrepreneurial-minded, work well with others in a highly collaborative project, exhibit team leadership potential, and are motivated by the prospect of launching the next generation of energy storage devices. General information about the Werner group at sites.bu.edu/wernerlab/. The initial appointment is for one year with an expected start date of October or November 2026 and the possibility of renewal on a yearly basis. The position is on site at the Boston University Charles River Campus in Boston MA. TYPICAL DUTIES Develop novel battery cell designs and architecture Electrochemical synthesis and deposition of materials Analyze the results of electrochemical experiments and characterization Perform and analyze flammability and safety tests on materials and devices Sealing/coating, contacting, and packaging of sensitive energy storage devices Iterate and improve on custom cell fabrication, from materials to assembly methods to cell design Prepare frequent technical reports, present results to research team and funding entities Required Skills PhD in Materials Science, Chemical Engineering, Chemistry, or a related engineering or physical science field is required. A strong experimental research background and abilities are required in at least two of the following areas Battery or electrochemical cell development and testing Hermetic sealing and packaging Metal plating (electroless and galvanic) Safety and flammability of materials, packaging, and/or batteries Electronic contacting and wiring of sensitive devices Experience working within a collaborative R&D team is desirable, being comfortable in an interdisciplinary research environment and team is a requirement. Excellent communication skills are required. Interest in entrepreneurship is strongly preferred. To Apply: Please send an email to jgwerner@bu.edu and include the following: (a) a cover letter, (b) a current CV, and (c) contact information for three scientists familiar with your work. PLEASE DO NOT APPLY TO THE BOSTON UNIVERSITY HUMAN RESOURCES WEBSITES. We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317403": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate, School of Public Health, Global Health",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate, School of Public Health, Global Health - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Postdoctoral Associate, School of Public Health, Global Health - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Postdoctoral Associate, School of Public Health, Global Health ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Postdoctoral Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937881014 Posted Date 10/14/2026 Minimum Salary $70,000.00 Maximum Salary $227,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Department of Global Health at the Boston University School of Public Health (BUSPH) is seeking a Postdoctoral Associate with strong training in epidemiology, causal inference, and the analysis of large healthcare datasets to join the research team of Dr. Alana T. Brennan, Associate Professor of Global Health and Epidemiology. The Postdoctoral Associate will play a central role in a newly funded NIH R01 examining the real-world effectiveness, safety, and cost-effectiveness of glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RAs) among people with HIV and type 2 diabetes. The study leverages Komodo Health's national longitudinal medical and pharmacy claims data linked with clinical and laboratory information to evaluate cardiovascular–kidney–metabolic (CKM) outcomes associated with GLP-1RA treatment. The multidisciplinary investigative team includes Co-Investigators Dr. Lawrence C. Long, Research Associate Professor of Global Health with expertise in health economics and applied health research, and Dr. Andrew C. Stokes, Associate Professor of Global Health with expertise in population health, epidemiologic methods, and causal inference. The R01 includes three complementary aims: characterizing real-world treatment patterns and early adverse events; estimating causal effects of GLP-1RAs versus other antidiabetes medications using target trial emulation; and evaluating long-term clinical and economic outcomes. The Postdoctoral Associate will primarily contribute to the epidemiologic and causal inference components of the study, with opportunities to collaborate across all three aims. This position is particularly well suited for an early-career epidemiologist interested in applying modern epidemiologic and causal inference methods to complex, large-scale real-world healthcare data. Prior experience in HIV, diabetes, or cardiometabolic research is not required. Required Skills Applicants should have: A doctoral degree (PhD, ScD, DrPH, or equivalent) in epidemiology or a closely related quantitative population health field by the anticipated start date. Strong methodological training in epidemiologic study design and causal inference. Demonstrated experience applying causal inference methods to observational data. Strong quantitative and statistical programming skills in Stata, SAS, and/or R. Experience with SQL for database management and querying large datasets is highly desirable. Experience analyzing large, longitudinal observational datasets. Strong scientific writing and oral communication skills. A record of peer-reviewed publications appropriate to career stage. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively within a multidisciplinary research team. Preferred Qualifications Particularly competitive applicants will have experience in one or more of the following: Target trial emulation, including new-user, active-comparator study designs. Advanced causal inference methods, including propensity score methods, inverse probability weighting, marginal structural models, and methods for time-varying exposures and confounding. Analysis of large U.S. administrative medical and pharmacy claims data or similarly complex longitudinal healthcare datasets. SQL, database management, code optimization, parallelization, and/or other approaches for efficiently analyzing very large healthcare datasets. Survival and time-to-event analysis, including competing-risk methods. Causal mediation analysis. Development of reproducible analytic workflows for large observational datasets. Training or experience in pharmacoepidemiology is a plus but is not required. Prior experience in HIV, diabetes, or cardiometabolic research is also a plus but is not required. We are particularly interested in candidates with strong epidemiologic methods and causal inference training who can apply these skills to complex, large-scale real-world healthcare data. To apply: Applicants should e-mail Dr. Alana Brennan (abrennan@bu.edu) with the subject heading: \"Postdoctoral Associate Position\". We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317336": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate, Sloane Epidemiology Center",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate, Sloane Epidemiology Center - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Postdoctoral Associate, Sloane Epidemiology Center - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Postdoctoral Associate, Sloane Epidemiology Center ﻿ Category Boston University Medical Campus --> Postdoctoral Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937800924 Posted Date 9/24/2026 Minimum Salary $67,500.00 Maximum Salary $70,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Slone Epidemiology Center at Boston University invites applications for a full-time Postdoctoral Associate position in health disparities and neuroepidemiology. We seek motivated candidates with relevant training and research experience to join the investigative team of the Strong Heart Study (SHS) programs on cerebrovascular and Alzheimer's diseases, with a particular focus on cognitive and psychological resilience. The SHS is a longitudinal cohort spanning 35+ years, representing adults from 13 American Indian Tribes and communities across 3 U.S. regions, and is the largest such cohort of vascular disease and related conditions in this population. This postdoctoral associate position will provide training and research opportunities within the field of neuroepidemiology, with a focus on cognitive aging, risk, and resilience; vascular and Alzheimer's dementias; and community-based participatory research. The Slone Epidemiology Center is located on the Boston University (BU) Medical Campus and includes faculty from both the School of Public Health and the School of Medicine. We have strong research collaborations across multiple BU departments and schools as well as the adjacent Boston Medical Center, a regional safety-net hospital. Additional research resources are available through Boston University Clinical and Translational Science Institute, the BU-BMC Research Center, the Biospecimen Archive Research Core, and more. Required Skills We are eager to recruit a colleague who supports our institutional commitment of ensuring that Boston University is inclusive, equitable, diverse, and a place where all constituents can thrive. We believe that diverse perspectives will enhance the quality of intellectual exchange and the creation of knowledge. Minimum qualifications include a doctoral degree in epidemiology or a related field (e.g., human biology, molecular biology, statistical genetics, biostatistics), with a track record of scholarship and scientific productivity in the form of peer-reviewed publications. Ideal candidates will demonstrate potential for extramural research funding. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to ongoing research programs as indicated above, and to develop independent research opportunities in collaboration with other SHS and Slone investigators. To Apply: Interested candidates are invited to send a cover letter stating interest in the position, curriculum vitae, a statement of current research interests and future research plans, and the names and contact information of three references. Materials should be emailed to Richard Vezina: rvezina@bu.edu. Application review will begin October 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/316641": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate, Translational Dental Medicine, School of Dental Medicine",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate, Translational Dental Medicine, School of Dental Medicine - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Postdoctoral Associate, Translational Dental Medicine, School of Dental Medicine - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Postdoctoral Associate, Translational Dental Medicine, School of Dental Medicine ﻿ Category Boston University Medical Campus --> Postdoctoral Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937240424 Posted Date 9/8/2026 Minimum Salary $67,500.00 Maximum Salary $75,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular Postdoctoral Associate Department of Translational Dental Medicine Boston University Henry M. Goldman School of Dental Medicine Boston University Medical Campus (BUMC) Position Type Full-time Postdoctoral Associate Appointment duration: 2–5 years, contingent upon performance and funding availability Position Overview Erdogan Lab, NIH-funded research laboratory in the Department of Translational Dental Medicine at Boston University Henry M. Goldman School of Dental Medicine, is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Associate to join an interdisciplinary research program investigating how neuro-immune responses and interactions shape pain, infection, and tissue repair in barrier tissues in the oral cavity. The lab operates at the interface of neurobiology, immunology, and microbiology, combining in vivo disease models, systems-level profiling, and translational approaches. Our goal is to define how sensory neurons respond and interact with immune and microbial signals in dental and oral tissues and to translate these insights into minimally invasive therapies and pain management strategies. Erdogan Lab is located within the Boston University Medical Campus and is highly collaborative, with active partnerships across Boston’s leading academic and medical institutions. Key Responsibilities Design, execute, and lead independent and collaborative, hypothesis-driven research at the intersection of neuroimmunology and oral biology Utilize in vivo models of dental injury, infection, and inflammation, and utilize chemogenetic approaches Integrate molecular, cellular, and systems-level approaches (e.g. flow cytometry, imaging, transcriptomics) Analyze complex datasets, including transcriptomic and microbiome data Lead projects from experimental design through publication Mentor trainees and contribute to a dynamic, team-oriented lab environment Required Skills Required Qualifications PhD, DDS/PhD or equivalent doctoral degree in immunology, neuroscience, microbiology, oral biology, or a related discipline Ability to work both independently and as part of a collaborative team A creative and rigorous scientist with a strong track record of research productivity Excited to tackle mechanistic, translational, cross-disciplinary questions Comfortable with (or eager to learn) in vivo biology and complex experimental systems Preferred Qualifications Experience in one or more of the following areas: Neuroimmunology or pain biology Host–microbe interactions or microbiome science Flow cytometry, advanced imaging, or spatial biology Transcriptomics Candidates with clinical training in dentistry (DDS/DMD) with substantial research experience are strongly encouraged to apply. Research Environment The Erdogan Lab is located at the Boston University Medical Campus and maintains active partnerships with neighboring academic and medical institutions in Boston. We foster a supportive, inclusive, and intellectually engaged lab culture that values curiosity, scientific rigor, and open collaboration To Apply: Please send a cover letter, CV, and contact information for three references to: Dr. Ozge Erdogan: oerdogan@bu.edu and Alecha Pantaleon: pantaleo@bu.edu Subject line: Postdoctoral Associate Application – Neuro-Immune Dental Research For additional inquiries, please contact Dr. Ozge Erdogan, email: oerdogan@bu.edu We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317243": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate, Virology, Immunology, & Microbiology, School of Medicine",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate, Virology, Immunology, & Microbiology, School of Medicine - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Postdoctoral Associate, Virology, Immunology, & Microbiology, School of Medicine - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Postdoctoral Associate, Virology, Immunology, & Microbiology, School of Medicine ﻿ Category Boston University Medical Campus --> Postdoctoral Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937620903 Posted Date 9/3/2026 Minimum Salary $67,500.00 Maximum Salary $70,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular One NIH-funded postdoctoral associate position is available in the upcoming laboratory of Dr. Yifei Liao in the Department of Virology, Immunology and Microbiology at Boston University Chobanian & Avedisian School of Medicine. The Liao laboratory focuses on tumor virology and related cancers/diseases, with a particular focus on epigenetic and metabolic controls of Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) lifecycle in lymphoproliferative diseases and carcinomas. The lab integrates cutting-edge CRISPR genome editing, transcriptomics, metabolomics, and proteomics approaches to study herpesvirus-host interactions in lymphocytes and epithelial cells. Required Skills The qualified candidate should have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree in Virology, Microbiology, Cancer Biology, Immunology or a related field. The candidate is expected to have extensive experiences in mammalian cell culture, flow cytometry, and DNA and protein related biochemical assays. The candidate with solid background in virology, 3D cell culture, or organoid model is particularly encouraged to apply. Strong time management and communication skills, and the ability to work both independently and collaboratively in a dynamic research environment are required. To Apply: For more information on the scientific background and the position, please contact Dr. Liao directly. To apply, please submit the following materials to Dr. Yifei Liao (yliao8@bwh.harvard.edu and liaoyifeicau@gmail.com): Cover letter (1-2 pages) describing research interests, experience, and career goals Curriculum vitae with publication list Contact information for three references We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317434": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Faculty Position at Wheelock College of Education & Human Development, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities",
      "description": "Assistant Professor Faculty Position at Wheelock College of Education & Human Development, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Assistant Professor Faculty Position at Wheelock College of Education & Human Development, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Assistant Professor Faculty Position at Wheelock College of Education & Human Development, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599938081015 Posted Date 10/15/2026 Minimum Salary $120,000.00 Maximum Salary $130,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Boston University Wheelock College of Education & Human Development invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position focused on transformative learning environments in urban settings . We seek a scholar with a commitment to interdisciplinary collaboration and a creative program of research , as well as the potential for securing external funding . This position is part of BU’s major investment in convergent research , bringing together bold ideas, deep expertise , and perspectives from across disciplines to take on society’s most critical challenges. The position is part of a University -wide cluster hire in Livable and Resilient Cities, which builds on BU’s strength in Ur b an Development & Resilience . Spanning anthropology, biology, civic data science, environmental health, journalism, learning sciences, political science, sociology, and urban affairs, the cluster draws together BU’s strengths in urban scholarship to advance new approaches to creating healthier, more equitable , and adaptable cities. Boston University Wheelock College of Education & Human Development is a leading institution dedicated to advancing learning and human development through equity-focused education, research, and community engagement. Its faculty conduct interdisciplinary research across topics such as education in formal and informal learning environments , child and adolescent development, and mental health to foster thriving communities. Situated in the heart of Boston, the college is further deepening its commitment to research on urban education and communities, with the goal of generating evidence that informs policies and practices to improve outcomes for children, families, and schools. Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the first universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. Please visit https://www.bu.edu/values/ to learn more about our shared values. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community through excellence in innovative scholarship , engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Required Skills Position Qualifications Candidates should have a PhD or equivalent degree in Education , Learning Sciences , or a related field, strong potential for external funding to support their program of research and scholarship, and a commitment to excellence and innovation in teaching, scholarship , and mentorship of students. Successful candidates will be expected to lead an independent research program; actively engage in collaborative research; teach and mentor students; and provide rank-appropriate service to the department, university, and profession. The salary allocated for th is role is $120,000 to $130,000. Final salary determination and rank placement will be based on the candidate's academic record, research background, years of qualifying experience, and a variety of other factors, including but not limited to licenses, specialty, departmental budgets, training and internal pay comparison. The stated hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Application Requirements Applicants should provide a personal statement that describes the candidate’s interest in both the convergent area of Livable and Resilient Cities and the department; a curriculum vitae; a research statement describing their research interests and experience; a teaching and mentoring statement detailing their approach to teaching and mentoring in an inclusive and diverse learning environment; and the names and contact information of 3 references at https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32946 Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, with a priority deadline of November 15. Applications will close November 30. Only applications submitted via the above AJO link will be reviewed. Boston University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender, age, national origin, ethnicity, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law or identified in the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Retaliation is also prohibited. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Under Massachusetts law, we may not require or administer a lie detector test as a condition of employment or continued employment. Boston University, consistent with the AAU’s Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment, conducts background checks for final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. As part of this process, we ask current and past employers of the last seven years whether there has been a substantiated finding of sexual misconduct under their policies. To move forward, final candidates must complete and sign an “Authorization to Release Information” form after receiving an offer to enable this background check. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317412": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Faculty Position in Systems Neuroscience Department of Biology, with a focus on Advancing Brain and Cognitive Health",
      "description": "Assistant Professor Faculty Position in Systems Neuroscience Department of Biology, with a focus on Advancing Brain and Cognitive Health - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Assistant Professor Faculty Position in Systems Neuroscience Department of Biology, with a focus on Advancing Brain and Cognitive Health - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Assistant Professor Faculty Position in Systems Neuroscience Department of Biology, with a focus on Advancing Brain and Cognitive Health ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937891015 Posted Date 10/15/2026 Minimum Salary $115,000.00 Maximum Salary $130,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Department of Biology at Boston University’s College of Arts and Sciences invites applications for tenure-track Assistant Professor positions in systems neuroscience and neurobiology of behavior. We seek scholars using cutting-edge approaches to investigate how neural circuits generate and regulate behavior across a broad range of organismal models. We are particularly interested in novel approaches for measuring and perturbing activity at large scales, the complementary application of molecular tools and functional recording, and their relationship to complex behaviors in naturalistic environments. These positions are part of BU’s major investment in convergent research, which brings together innovative ideas, deep disciplinary expertise, and diverse perspectives to address complex challenges in brain and cognitive health. We particularly seek candidates whose research will complement other areas represented in the convergent search, including human neuroimaging, computational neuroscience, brain-computer interfaces, and rehabilitation applications. The position is part of a University-wide cluster hire in Advancing Brain and Cognitive Health, which builds on BU’s strength in Health Across the Lifespan. The cluster hire draws on BU’s strengths in neuroscience, engineering, computation, and AI to pursue transformative research questions, advance our understanding of neural information processing, and develop new approaches to neurological and psychiatric disease. This cluster hiring initiative is recruiting faculty in departments spanning biology, biomedical engineering, electrical and computer engineering, health sciences, mathematics and statistics, and psychological and brain sciences. Boston University’s broader Neuroscience community offers extensive opportunities for collaboration across neuroscience, medicine, public health, engineering, data science, and psychology, fostering innovative approaches to understanding and preventing neurological disease, promoting cognitive resilience, improving treatment and care, reducing health inequities, and enhancing quality of life across the lifespan. Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the first universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. Please visit https://www.bu.edu/values/ to learn more about our shared values. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship, engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Required Skills Position Qualifications Candidates should have a PhD or equivalent degre e in b iology or a related field, demonstrated ability to obtain external funding to support their program of research and scholarship , and a commit ment to excellence and innovation in teaching, education, and mentorship of students. Successful candidates will be expected to lead an independent research program; actively engage in collaborative research; teach and mentor students; and provide rank-appropriate service to the department, university, and profession. The salary allocated for these roles is $115,000 to $130,000 . Fin al salary determination and rank placement will be based on the candidate's academic record, research background, years of qualifying experience, and a variety of other factors, including but not limited to licenses, specialty, departmental budgets, training and internal pay comparison. The stated hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Application Requirements Applicants should provide a personal statement that describes their interest in both the convergent area of Advancing Brain and Cognitive Health and the department; a curriculum vitae; a research statement describing their research interests and experience; a teaching and mentoring statement detailing their approach to teaching and mentoring in an inclusive and diverse learning environment ; and letters of recommendation from 3 references at https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32914 Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, with a priority deadline of November 15. Applications will close November 30. Only applications submitted via the above AJO link will be reviewed. Boston University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender, age, national origin, ethnicity, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law or identified in the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Retaliation is also prohibited. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Under Massachusetts law, we may not require or administer a lie detector test as a condition of employment or continued employment. Boston University, consistent with the AAU’s Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment, conducts background checks for final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. As part of this process, we ask current and past employers of the last seven years whether there has been a substantiated finding of sexual misconduct under their policies. To move forward, final candidates must complete and sign an “Authorization to Release Information” form after receiving an offer to enable this background check. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317426": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Faculty Position in the Department of Anthropology, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities",
      "description": "Assistant Professor Faculty Position in the Department of Anthropology, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Assistant Professor Faculty Position in the Department of Anthropology, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Assistant Professor Faculty Position in the Department of Anthropology, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599938011015 Posted Date 10/15/2026 Minimum Salary $100,000.00 Maximum Salary $115,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Department of Anthropology at Boston University’s College of Arts and Sciences invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position whose work focuses on livable and resilient cities. Specifically, we seek an anthropological archaeologist specializing in comparative analyses of urban resilience and preservation. Qualified applicants should have expertise in the analysis of large datasets dealing with urban centers over deep time, with particular implications for understanding issues of quality of life and resiliency, and/or dealing with the tangible heritage of archaeological and historical cities as part of contemporary ones. This position is part of BU’s major investment in convergent research , bringing together bold ideas, deep expertise , and perspectives from across disciplines to take on society’s most critical challenges. The position is part of a University -wide cluster hire in Livable and Resilient Cities, which builds on BU’s strength in Urban Development & Resilience . Spanning anthropology, biology, civic data science, environmental health, journalism, learning sciences, political science, sociology, and urban affairs, the cluster draws together BU’s strengths in urban scholarship to advance new approaches to creating healthier, more equitable , and adaptable cities. The Department of Anthropology at Boston University is a globally engaged, interdisciplinary community of scholars whose research explores the relationships among people, environments, cultures, and institutions across time and space. Building on strengths in sociocultural anthropology, archaeology, biological anthropology, and environmental anthropology, the department offers an exceptional environment for scholars interested in how cities are shaped by migration, inequality, environmental change, governance, infrastructure, and community life, as well as how urban residents adapt to and transform rapidly changing social and ecological conditions. Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the first universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. Please visit https://www.bu.edu/values/ to learn more about our shared values. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship , engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Required Skills Position Qualifications Candidates should have a PhD or equivalent degree in Anthropology or a related field, a demonstrated ability to lead an independent research program, a nd a commitment to excellence and innovation in teaching, education, and mentorship of students. Successful cand idates will be expected to lead an independent research program; actively engage in collaborative research; teach and mentor students; and provide rank-appropriate service to the department, university, and profession. The salary allocated for th is role i s $1 00 ,000 to $1 15 ,000 . Final salary determination and rank placement will be based on the candidate's academic record, research background, years of qualifying experience, and a variety of other factors, including but no t limited to licenses, specialty, departmental budgets, training and internal pay comparison. The stated hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Application Requirements Applicants should provide a personal statement that describes the candidate’s interest in both the convergent area of Livable and Resilient Cities and the department; a curriculum vitae; a research statement describing their research interests and experience; a teaching and mentoring statement detailing their approach to teaching and mentoring in an inclusive and diverse learning environment; and the names and contact information of 3 references at https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32941 Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, with a priority deadline of November 15. Applications will close November 30. Only applications submitted via the abov e AJO link will be reviewed. Boston University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender, age, national origin, ethnicity, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law or identified in the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Retaliation is also prohibited. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Under Massachusetts law, we may not require or administer a lie detector test as a condition of employment or continued employment. Boston University, consistent with the AAU’s Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment, conducts background checks for final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. As part of this process, we ask current and past employers of the last seven years whether there has been a substantiated finding of sexual misconduct under their policies. To move forward, final candidates must complete and sign an “Authorization to Release Information” form after receiving an offer to enable this background check. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
