    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317417": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Faculty Position in the Department of Psychological and Brain Sciences, with a focus on Advancing Brain and Cognitive Health",
      "description": "Assistant Professor Faculty Position in the Department of Psychological and Brain Sciences, with a focus on Advancing Brain and Cognitive Health - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Assistant Professor Faculty Position in the Department of Psychological and Brain Sciences, with a focus on Advancing Brain and Cognitive Health - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Assistant Professor Faculty Position in the Department of Psychological and Brain Sciences, with a focus on Advancing Brain and Cognitive Health ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937931015 Posted Date 10/15/2026 Minimum Salary $115,000.00 Maximum Salary $130,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Department of Psychological & Brain Sciences at Boston University’s College of Arts and Sciences inv ites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in human cognitive neuroscience . We seek scholars with strong quantitative and computational expertise and a foundational understanding of neuroanatomy and systems neuroscience. This position is part of BU’s major investment in convergent research , bringing together bold ideas, deep expertise , and perspectives from across disciplines to take on society’s most critical challenges. The position is part of a University -wide cluster hire in Advancing Brain and Cognitive Health, which builds on BU’s strength in Health Across the Lifespan . Spanning biology, biomedical engineering, electrical and computer engineering, health sciences, mathematics and statistics, and psychological and brain sciences, the cluster hire draws on BU’s strengths in neuroscience, engineering, computation, and AI to pursue transformative research questions, advance our understanding of neural information processing, and develop new approaches to neurological and psychiatric disease. T his position within t he Department of Psychological & Brain Sciences at Boston University will sit in the Brain, Behavior and Cognition program , whi ch has strong ties to the undergraduate and graduate neuroscience training programs, the Center for Systems Neuroscience, and Cognitive Neuroimaging Center. The ideal candidate will be work ing in h uman cognitive neuroscience with a strong quantitative and computational background , and solid foundational understanding of neuroanatomy and systems neuroscience. The ideal candidate will also make use of the shared research infrastructure in the Cognitive Neuroimaging Center. We also note that there are numerous ‘big data’ initiatives with in cognitive neuroscience, and this hire could contribute to the convergent theme of AI, Data Science and Computing. We particularly emphasize candidates whose research will interact with other hires in the convergent search, including faculty in computational neuroscience , brain-computer interface, clinical rehabilitation applications and animal systems neuroscience. Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the first universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. Please visit https://www.bu.edu/values/ to learn more about our shared values. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship , engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Required Skills Position Qualifications Candidates should have a PhD or equivalent degree in Neuroscience, Psychology, or a related field, the ability to obtain external funding to support their program of research and scholarship, and a commitment to excellence and innovation in teaching, education, and mentorship of students. Successful candidates will be expected to lead an independent research program; actively engage in collaborative research; teach and mentor students; and provide rank-appropriate service to the department, university, and profession. The salary allocated for these roles is $115,000 to $130,000 . Final salary determination and rank placement will be based on the candidate's academic record, research background, years of qualifying experience, and a variety of other factors, including but not limited to licenses, specialty, departmental budgets, training and internal pay comparison. The stated hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Application Requirements Applicants should provide a personal statement that describes the ir interest in both the convergent area of Advancing Brain and Cognitive Health and the department; curriculum vitae; a research statement describing their research interests and experience; a teaching and mentoring statement detailing their approach to teaching and mentoring in an inclusive and diverse learning environment; and letters of recommendation from 3 references at https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32913 Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, with a priority deadline of November 15. Applications will close November 30. Only applications submitted via the above AJO link will be reviewed. Boston University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender, age, national origin, ethnicity, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law or identified in the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Retaliation is also prohibited. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Under Massachusetts law, we may not require or administer a lie detector test as a condition of employment or continued employment. Boston University, consistent with the AAU’s Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment, conducts background checks for final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. As part of this process, we ask current and past employers of the last seven years whether there has been a substantiated finding of sexual misconduct under their policies. To move forward, final candidates must complete and sign an “Authorization to Release Information” form after receiving an offer to enable this background check. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317433": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Faculty Position in the Department of Sociology, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities",
      "description": "Assistant Professor Faculty Position in the Department of Sociology, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Assistant Professor Faculty Position in the Department of Sociology, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Assistant Professor Faculty Position in the Department of Sociology, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599938071015 Posted Date 10/15/2026 Minimum Salary $115,000.00 Maximum Salary $130,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Department of Sociology at the Boston U niversity College of Arts and Sciences invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in livable and resilient cities. We seek applicants whose research addresses complex urban questions and discover innovative solutions theoretically, conceptually, and/or in terms of policy and practice. Areas of interest include urban inequality, poverty, segregation, housing and equity, urban analytics, urban environment al challenges , urban health, crime, law and policing in urban contexts , and urban futures. We are particularly interested in scholars with expertise in AI, quantitative , and computational approaches and who rely on spatial analysis and /or mixed methods. This position is part of BU’s major investment in convergent research , bringing together bold ideas, deep expertise , and perspectives from across disciplines to take on society’s most critical challenges. The position is part of a University -wide cluster hire in Livable and Resilient Cities, which builds on BU’s strength in Urban Development & Resilience . Spanning anthropology, biology, civic data science, environmental health, journalism, learning sciences, political science, sociology, and urban affairs, the cluster draws together BU’s strengths in urban scholarship to advance new approaches to creating healthier, more equitable , and adaptable cities. The Department of Sociology fosters teaching and research that expands knowledge of human social behavior. Faculty research examines how housing, segregation, education, environmental change, public health, violence, neighborhood transformation , and urban policy shape opportunity and well-being in cities, advancing our understanding of urban life and inequality. Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the first universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. Please visit https://www.bu.edu/values/ to learn more about our shared values. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship , engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Required Skills Position Qualifications Candidates should have a PhD or equivalent degree in Sociology or a related field, and a commitment to excellence and innovation in teaching, education, and mentorship of students. Successful candidates will be expected to lead an independent research program; actively engage in collaborative research; teach and mentor students; and provide rank-appropriate service to the department, university, and profession. The salary allocated for th is role is $115,000 to $130,000 . Final salary determination and rank placement will be based on the candidate's academic record, research background, years of qualifying experience, and a variety of other factors, including but not limited to licenses, specialty, departmental budgets, training and internal pay comparison. The stated hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Application Requirements Applicants should provide a personal statement that describes the candidate’s interest in both the convergent area of Livable and Resilient Cities and the department; a curriculum vitae; a research statement describing their research interests and experience; a teaching and mentoring statement detailing their approach to teaching and mentoring in an inclusive and diverse learning environment; and the names and contact information of 3 references at https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32945 Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, with a priority deadline of November 15. Applications will close November 30. Only applications submitted via the above AJO link will be reviewed. Boston University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender, age, national origin, ethnicity, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law or identified in the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Retaliation is also prohibited. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Under Massachusetts law, we may not require or administer a lie detector test as a condition of employment or continued employment. Boston University, consistent with the AAU’s Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment, conducts background checks for final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. As part of this process, we ask current and past employers of the last seven years whether there has been a substantiated finding of sexual misconduct under their policies. To move forward, final candidates must complete and sign an “Authorization to Release Information” form after receiving an offer to enable this background check. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
