      "title": "Assistant Professor Faculty Positions in the Department of Surgery, with a focus on The Future of Healthy Aging",
      "description": "Assistant Professor Faculty Positions in the Department of Surgery, with a focus on The Future of Healthy Aging - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Assistant Professor Faculty Positions in the Department of Surgery, with a focus on The Future of Healthy Aging - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Assistant Professor Faculty Positions in the Department of Surgery, with a focus on The Future of Healthy Aging ﻿ Category Boston University Medical Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599937991015 Posted Date 10/15/2026 Minimum Salary $165,000.00 Maximum Salary $180,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Department of Surgery at the Boston University Chobanian & Avedisian School of Medicine invites applications for an Assistant Professor position in surgical epidemiology or health services research. This position is part of BU’s major investment in convergent research , bringing together bold ideas, deep expertise , and perspectives from across disciplines to take on society’s most critical challenges. We seek scholars whose work examines how structural and social determinants of health influence surgical care and outcomes, particularly among older adults and populations served by safety-net health systems. Using population science approaches, large-scale clinical and health-system data, patient-centered clinical research, and emerging AI-enabled methods, the successful candidate will investigate disparities in surgical risk, access to and delivery of care, outcomes, and long-term recovery among older adults. Particular emphasis will be placed on identifying modifiable factors and developing evidence-based approaches to reduce disparities and improve outcomes. The position will advance interdisciplinary research at the intersection of healthy aging, health equity, and surgical care. This cluster hire builds on BU’s strength in Health Across the Lifespan , an area of significant expertise and opportunity across the University. BU is home to renowned studies of aging including the Framingham Heart Study and New England Centenarian Study , as well as innovative interdisciplinary research centers focused on the demography and economics of population aging , Alzheimer’s disease and related dementias , the biology of aging , and more. The Future of Healthy Aging draws on this foundation, bringing together faculty from anthropology, biology, business, communications, epidemiology, law, physical therapy, sociology, and surgery to pursue transformative research questions about how we age and the biological, cultural, environmental, technological, legal, and social factors that shape healthy aging as well as the structural factors that prevent some from aging well. The cluster hire will add new ideas , methods, and perspectives to this work, connecting fundamental discovery, population health, and translational research to advance healthier aging across the life course and to meet the challenges of an aging global population. The Department of Surgery is a dynamic and collaborative research community dedicated to advancing health and well-being across the lifespan through innovations in surgical science, clinical care, and translational research. Faculty pursue pioneering work in areas including cancer, cardiovascular disease, trauma, transplantation, regenerative medicine, health services research, and surgical outcomes, with a shared commitment to advancing health equity and improving both longevity and quality of life. Leveraging the d epartment’s diverse patient population and safety-net clinical environment, faculty integrate multidisciplinary research, population science, and emerging data-driven approaches to address complex surgical and health challenges across the lifespan. Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the first universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. Please visit https://www.bu.edu/values/ to learn more about our shared values. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship , engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Required Skills Position Qualifications Candidates should have a MD, PhD , or equivalent degree in Surgery or a related field, active external funding to support their program of research and scholarship, and a commitment to excellence and innovation in teaching, education, and mentorship of students. Successful candidates will be expected to lead an independent research program; actively engage in collaborative research; teach and mentor students; and provide rank-appropriate service to the department, university, and profession. The salary allocated for these roles is $165,000 to $180,000 . Fin al salary determination and rank placement will be based on the candidate's academic record, research background, years of qualifying experience, and a variety of other factors, including but not limited to licenses, specialty, departmental budgets, training and internal pay comparison. The stated hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Application Requirements Applicants should provide a personal statement that describes the ir interest in both the convergent area of The Future of Healthy Aging and the department ; curriculum vitae ; a research statement describing their research interests and experience ; a teaching and mentoring statement detailing their approach to teaching and mentoring in an inclusive and diverse learning environment; and the names and contact information of 3 references at https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32924 . Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, with a priority deadline of November 15. Applications will close November 30. Only applications submitted via the above AJO link will be reviewed. Boston University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender, age, national origin, ethnicity, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law or identified in the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Retaliation is also prohibited. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Under Massachusetts law, we may not require or administer a lie detector test as a condition of employment or continued employment. Boston University, consistent with the AAU’s Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment, conducts background checks for final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. As part of this process, we ask current and past employers of the last seven years whether there has been a substantiated finding of sexual misconduct under their policies. To move forward, final candidates must complete and sign an “Authorization to Release Information” form after receiving an offer to enable this background check. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317428": {
      "title": "Associate Professor Faculty Position in the School of Law, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities",
      "description": "Associate Professor Faculty Position in the School of Law, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Associate Professor Faculty Position in the School of Law, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Associate Professor Faculty Position in the School of Law, with a focus on Livable and Resilient Cities ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599938031015 Posted Date 10/15/2026 Minimum Salary $175,000.00 Maximum Salary $225,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The School of Law at Boston University invites applications for a tenure-track Associate Professor position whose work is at the intersection of tax law and livable and resilient cities. This position is part of BU’s major investment in convergent research , bringing together bold ideas, deep expertise , and perspectives from across disciplines to take on society’s most critical challenges. We seek scholars examining how law and legal institutions shape the experience of cities and urban environments , with particular interest in tax law and its intersections with family, caregiving, inequality, and economic security. Areas of interest may also include discrimination, disability, healthcare decision-making, and personal autonomy. The successful candidate will contribute new perspectives on how legal structures can create or address disparities in economic, physical, and cognitive well-being across the life course . The position is part of a University -wide cluster hire in Livable and Resilient Cities, which builds on BU’s strength in Urban Development & Resilience . Spanning anthropology, biology, civic data science, environmental health, journalism, learning sciences, political science, sociology, and urban affairs, the cluster draws together BU’s strengths in urban scholarship to advance new approaches to creating healthier, more equitable , and adaptable cities. Boston University School of Law is a nationally recognized center for legal scholarship addressing complex challenges affecting health, well-being, and quality of life across the lifespan, with its Health Law program currently ranked No. 2 in the nation by U.S. News & World Report. Faculty conduct influential research on health law, bioethics, disability rights, aging, reproductive justice, public health, data privacy, and the regulation of emerging technologies, helping to shape law and policy at local, national, and global levels. Through interdisciplinary collaboration with scholars across medicine, public health, social work, and the sciences, BU Law advances evidence-based solutions that promote equitable access to care, protect vulnerable populations, and strengthen the legal and policy frameworks that support healthier lives and communities. Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the first universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. Please visit https://www.bu.edu/values/ to learn more about our shared values. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship , engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Required Skills Position Qualifications Candidates should have a JD or equivalent degree in Law or a related field, a demonstrated ability to lead an independent research program , and a commitment to excellence and innovation in teaching, education, and mentorship of students. Successful candidates will be expected to actively engage in collaborative research; teach and mentor students; and provide rank-appropriate service to the department, university, and profession. The salary allocated for these roles is $175,000 to $225,000. Final salary determination and rank placement will be based on the candidate's academic record, research background, years of qualifying experience, and a variety of other factors, including but not limited to licenses, specialty, departmental budgets, training and internal pay comparison. The stated hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Application Requirements Applicants should provide a personal statement that describes the candidate’s interest in both the convergent area of Livable and Resilient Cities and the department; a curriculum vitae; a research statement describing their research interests and experience; a teaching and mentoring statement detailing their approach to teaching and mentoring in an inclusive and diverse learning environment; and the names and contact information of 3 references at https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32922 Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, with a priority deadline of November 15. Applications will close November 30. Only applications submitted via the above AJO link will be reviewed. Boston University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender, age, national origin, ethnicity, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law or identified in the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Retaliation is also prohibited. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Under Massachusetts law, we may not require or administer a lie detector test as a condition of employment or continued employment. Boston University, consistent with the AAU’s Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment, conducts background checks for final candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. As part of this process, we ask current and past employers of the last seven years whether there has been a substantiated finding of sexual misconduct under their policies. To move forward, final candidates must complete and sign an “Authorization to Release Information” form after receiving an offer to enable this background check. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
