    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317461": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Advanced Estate Planning, Boston University School of Law, Graduate Tax",
      "description": "Lecturer, Advanced Estate Planning, Boston University School of Law, Graduate Tax - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Lecturer, Advanced Estate Planning, Boston University School of Law, Graduate Tax - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Lecturer, Advanced Estate Planning, Boston University School of Law, Graduate Tax ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599938121021 Posted Date 10/21/2026 Minimum Salary $5,000.00 Maximum Salary $7,250.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Part-Time The Graduate Tax Program at Boston University School of Law seeks an experienced instructor to teach Advanced Estate Planning for the Spring or Summer 2027 semester. This is a 2-credit course which is taught as a 2-hour class, meeting once per week for 13 weeks. The spring semester meets in-person with some students also joining over Zoom. The summer semester is fully online and all teaching will be done over Zoom. Required Skills Prior experience teaching in a law school and practice experience in advanced estate planning is strongly preferred. Candidates must be comfortable teaching through Zoom and willing to learn and utilize other educational technology tools, such as the Blackboard learning management system. To apply: Applicants should email a letter of interest and a resume to the Assistant Dean for Graduate Programs at ricec@bu.edu ALL APPLICATIONS MUST BE RECEIVED PRIOR TO DECEMBER 1, 2026. We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317462": {
      "title": "Professor of Creative Writing, College of Arts and Sciences, English Department",
      "description": "Professor of Creative Writing, College of Arts and Sciences, English Department - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Professor of Creative Writing, College of Arts and Sciences, English Department - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Professor of Creative Writing, College of Arts and Sciences, English Department ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599938131021 Posted Date 10/21/2026 Minimum Salary $150,000.00 Maximum Salary $220,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular Boston University invites applications for a faculty position at the rank of Professor in the CAS English Department and Creative Writing Program (CWP). We are looking for a nationally or internationally known poet (writing in English) with a possible specialty in a second literary genre ( e.g. fiction, creative nonfiction, or literary translation). The successful candidate will carry a standard four-course load and will teach both creative writing workshops and literature courses at both the UG and MFA/graduate level, participating in the recruitment and mentoring of MFA poets. The candidate will also serve as Director of the CWP on a rotating basis (administrative experience required ), with a course reduction for this service. MFA and/or Ph.D. degree (or equivalent expertise ) required . Prior teaching experience at both the undergraduate and graduate (MFA) level is a plus. The successful candidate will be considered for appointment at the rank of Full Professor with tenure. Salary range: $150,000 – $220,000. Priority consideration will be given to applications received by November 15, 2026 and applications received after December 1, 2026 will not be considered. Application Instructions: To apply, please submit cover letter; CV; writing sample (max. 20 pages); teaching statement; statement about applicant’s own creative practice and its relation to teaching; and three letters of reference, to ensearch@bu.edu . Since its founding in 1839, Boston University has embraced a mission of openness and access, becoming one of the ﬁrst universities in the nation to welcome all students of ability regardless of race, gender, or creed. That tradition shapes our enduring commitment to academic freedom, free inquiry, and the robust exchange of ideas. Please visit https://www.bu.edu/values/ to learn more about our shared values. We seek scholars who will contribute to our community of teachers and researchers through excellence in innovative scholarship , engaged teaching, and participation in the intellectual life of the University. Boston University provides an environment where faculty and students work together across disciplines, perspectives, and experiences to advance knowledge and address society’s most pressing challenges. Boston University is an equal opportunity employer. All qualiﬁed applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, gender, age, national origin, ethnicity, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law or identiﬁed in the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Retaliation is also prohibited. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. Under Massachusetts law, we may not require or administer a lie detector test as a condition of employment or continued employment. Boston University, consistent with the AAU’s Principles on Preventing Sexual Harassment, conducts background checks for ﬁnal candidates for certain faculty and staff positions. As part of this process, we ask current and past employers of the last seven years whether there has been a substantiated ﬁnding of sexual misconduct under their policies. To move forward, ﬁnal candidates must complete and sign an “Authorization to Release Information” form after receiving an offer to enable this background check. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317484": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Associate, School of Public Health, Department of Biostatistics",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Associate, School of Public Health, Department of Biostatistics - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Post Doctoral Associate, School of Public Health, Department of Biostatistics - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Post Doctoral Associate, School of Public Health, Department of Biostatistics ﻿ Category Boston University Medical Campus --> Postdoctoral Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599938221023 Posted Date 10/23/2026 Minimum Salary $82,500.00 Maximum Salary $127,500.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular Position Information. Two-year, renewable post-doctoral position to work in the areas of statistical genetics and genomics. This position will allow for the development and implementation of innovative statistical and machine learning approaches for analysis of genetics and genomics data. We are particularly interested in applicants who have experience with methods and analysis of big data, especially genetics and genomics datasets. However, we will consider qualified applicants with experience in other related research areas. Required Skills The successful applicant is expected to participate actively both in collaborative research projects and applied methodological research. The position also has potential for teaching opportunities for interested applicants. Successful applicants are highly motivated, dependable, and have excellent communication and writing skills. Strong programming skills are required in R and/or SAS. Additional background with high-level programming languages such as Python, and experience with Linux cluster computing environment is strongly preferred. Candidates should hold a PhD or equivalent doctoral degree in statistics, biostatistics, or closely related quantitative fields with strong computing skills. Applications will be considered until the position is filled. Application Instructions Interested applicants should send the following materials to Dr. Ching-Ti Liu (ctliu@bu.edu) with the subject line “Application for Postdoctoral Associate Position: [Your Name]”: a curriculum vitae, a cover letter detailing research experience, career goals, and how your research interests and long-term professional objectives align with this position, and contact information for three references. We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color , natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317479": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate, Computational Biomedicine",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate, Computational Biomedicine - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Postdoctoral Associate, Computational Biomedicine - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Postdoctoral Associate, Computational Biomedicine ﻿ Category Boston University Medical Campus --> Postdoctoral Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599938201023 Posted Date 10/23/2026 Minimum Salary $67,500.00 Maximum Salary $70,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular The Beane Lab within the section of computational biomedicine at Boston University Chobanian & Avedisian School of Medicine is seeking a highly motivated computational postdoctoral associate to lead the analysis of single-cell and spatial transcriptomic data across two translational research programs in lung and urethral disease. The successful candidate will join a collaborative group of computational biologists, bench scientists, pathologists, and clinicians, and will take intellectual ownership of projects from data processing through biological interpretation and publication. Required Skills Required PhD (or equivalent) in computational biology, bioinformatics, genomics, biostatistics, systems biology, or a related quantitative field, obtained within the last [3] years or expected before the start date. Hands-on experience analyzing single-cell and/or spatial transcriptomic data, with a first-author publication or preprint demonstrating this work. Proficiency in R and/or Python and standard analysis frameworks (e.g., Seurat, Bioconductor, scanpy, squidpy, scvi-tools), and comfort working in a Linux/high-performance computing environment. Working knowledge of, or a strong willingness to learn, the underlying biology, including epithelial and immune cell biology, tissue histology, and the pathophysiology of lung and urethral disease. Excellent written and verbal communication skills in English and the ability to work both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. Preferred Background in cancer biology, immunology, pulmonary or urologic disease, or tissue pathology. Experience with multimodal data integration, spatial neighborhood analysis, or machine learning applied to genomic or imaging data. Experience with workflow management (e.g., Nextflow, Snakemake), containers, and version control (Git). Application Instructions: Please submit the following to Dr. Jennifer Beane (jbeane@bu.edu) with \"Postdoctoral Associate – Single-Cell and Spatial Transcriptomics\" in the subject line: a cover letter (one page) describing your research experience, your interest in the position, and how your background prepares you to work at the interface of computation and lung or urethral biology; a current CV including publications and links to code (e.g., GitHub); and names and contact information for three references. We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color , natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    },
    "https://jobs.silkroad.com/BU/Faculty/jobs/317491": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor of Counseling Psychology, Wheelock College of Education and Human Development",
      "description": "Associate or Full Professor of Counseling Psychology, Wheelock College of Education and Human Development - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Associate or Full Professor of Counseling Psychology, Wheelock College of Education and Human Development - Boston, Massachusetts, United States - Boston University Skip Navigation Menu Jobs My Account Loading... search chevron_left Boston University Jobs Apply Associate or Full Professor of Counseling Psychology, Wheelock College of Education and Human Development ﻿ Category Charles River Campus --> Faculty Job Location Boston, Massachusetts, United States Tracking Code 26599938231027 Posted Date 10/27/2026 Minimum Salary $120,000.00 Maximum Salary $170,000.00 The salary of the finalist selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to departmental budgets, qualifications, experience, education, licenses, specialty, training and internal pay comparison. The above hiring range represents the University's good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. Position Type Full-Time/Regular Associate or Full Professor of Counseling Psychology The department of Counseling Psychology and Applied Human Development within the Boston University Wheelock College of Education and Human Development invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position as Associate or Full Professor in Counseling Psychology. Competitive candidates will have a significant program of research and the ability to contribute to the practice and teaching of core courses in our APA-Approved Counseling Psychology Ph.D. program and our Ed.M. Counseling program that includes three strands of specialization: Sport Psychology, Child and Adolescent Mental Health, and School Counseling. The ideal candidate will be a scholar whose research expertise focuses on an area of growing national need and directly aligned with BU Wheelock’s strategic priorities, which is the trauma, resilience, healing, and thriving among marginalized populations. We are particularly interested in a scholar whose research focuses on the lived experiences of Black/African Americans, which could include mental health disparities, trauma-informed practices, radical healing, resilience and thriving, equity and social justice, mental health interventions in sport and exercise settings, and/or career and vocational interventions. The Counseling Psychology and Applied Human Development Department, where the Counseling programs are housed, believes that the cultural and social diversity of our faculty, staff, and students is vitally important to the distinction and excellence of our research and academic programs. We encourage applicants who support our institutional commitment to equity, diversity, and belonging and to being an inclusive university where all constituents can thrive. Competitive candidates will demonstrate a background of engaged and collaborative scholarship, practice, and teaching, as well as a commitment to curricula that deeply engages with the unique histories, experiences, challenges and resilience of marginalized individuals. Responsibilities Teach graduate-level courses related to our doctoral program in counseling psychology and master’s degree program in counseling. Maintain a strong program of grant-funded research Supervise graduate students in practicum placements and independent research. Be an engaged citizen in providing service to the department, college/university, and the profession. Salary Range: The expected base pay range for this position is $120,000 - $170,000. This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment. Additional compensation up to three months may be earned for activities including but not limited to funded research and summer teaching. This position begins 07/01/2027. Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience. Review of applications will begin December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries may be directed to the Chair of the Search Committee: Dr. Jennifer Greif Green (jggreen@bu.edu) Required Skills Required Qualifications Graduate from an APA-Accredited Psychology program Preferred Qualifications Graduate from an APA-Accredited Counseling Psychology program Licensed or license-eligible psychologist (HSP) in Massachusetts Supervision experience Demonstrated ability to attract extramural funding Application Materials: Cover letter including statements of teaching, research, and service and describing suitability for the position. Candidates who substantively address their experiences, strengths, and opportunities for growth in building equitable and just education environments in partnership with schools and communities will receive strong consideration. Curriculum vitae Three recent publications or scholarly writing samples Contact information for three recommendations (who will be contacted at the finalist stage) Applications should be submitted electronically only at: https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33212 We are an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color , natural or protective hairstyle, religion, sex, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, sexual orientation, gender identity, genetic information, military service, pregnancy or pregnancy-related condition, or because of marital, parental, or veteran status. We are a VEVRAA Federal Contractor. This position is located in Boston, MA. View the Google Map in full screen . Apply close"
    }
